title The door that opened on the seat it replaced, and the day that began empty
prompt the standing baton -- take the next agent-doable lap in the auditing lane, reds first
rota lap 4119, row 4 by git rev-list --count HEAD -- EARTH, Mercury, the concrete, and its sense is aroma: take the fact in at the door, ahead of the argument. Read the threshold, then the clock and the mark, Two Rooms, and the marked value. Grass last read row 4 at lap 4089, on the previous day, so the count and the hand agree.
think The threshold named this lap before it had a subject, and it did so by being wrong. Its own Cardinal section cited context/specs/20260627-102012_one-clock-naming-law.md while the grid seats foundations/20260905-154954_the-clock-and-the-mark.md, changed two days earlier. The row that teaches a lap to trust the fact read at the door was routing every reader through a door onto the wrong room.
think I measured before arguing, which is the row's own discipline. Fifteen seats, fourteen agreeing, one not -- and the one was in the row being read that morning. All twenty grid paths exist, so the existence half was healthy and only the agreement half had drifted.
think The sharp part is that no standing guard could have caught it. readme_reach_scan follows Markdown links, and the grid's fifteen seat cells are backticked bare paths; but that is only half the reason. The other half is that BOTH paths resolve. The threshold cited a live, canonical file. A link wall reads whether a door opens, and this was a door opening onto the room next to the one it named.
think Then the cold roster answered 2 red before I could book any of that, and reds-first took the lap. Both reds were one root: rota_declared, seated the previous day, refuses when the OPEN day has no shelf -- and 20260907 was its first day rollover. standing_equipment reddened purely for recording the run, runs_red=1 runs_red_self=0.
think I reproduced it in one command rather than reasoning about it: sh tools/fixtures/r/rota_declared_scan.sh answered refused: no day shelf at session-logs/date/20260907, exit 2. The guard's own header warns that a gate unable to tell an honest lap from a skipped one reds on honest work, which is the gate somebody turns off. It reddened on honest work on its second day.
think The discriminator took a minute to find and is exact: whose day it is. A day the CALLER names and has no shelf is a question about a day that never ran, and refuses. The LIVE day may stand empty, because the lap asking is the one about to write the first log into it. The corpus REDS %170 protects is the ROOM rather than the day, so an absent session-logs/date refuses as before.
think Reading the roster's own evidence file turned up a second thing I had not gone looking for. construction/standing-equipment-reds/rota_declared.txt held the assertion line with an EMPTY reason. I probed Rishi on metal and my first reading was wrong: run returns out AND err separately. What throws the reason away is a witness reporting ${scan.out} alone, which is a different and cheaper fault than the one I described. Corrected on the rebase, where a peer's own repair showed me the field existed.
obs The cost is fleet-wide and measured rather than estimated. A red roster withholds the --scoped receipt and receipts chain from full greens alone, so every ship's next pass falls back to a FULL cold roster: 896 seconds here. Eight ships, once a day, is eight reds and eight full passes -- for a shelf that had simply not been written yet.
obs I built the grid guard before the roster finished, in /tmp, because the runner digests the tree at open and close and refuses tree_moved. Reading is free during a pass; writing is not. That constraint turned out to be useful rather than costly -- I spent the wait measuring instead of editing.
obs The new control is born with the check REDS %519 asks for: every plant is compared with cmp -s against the file it replaced and answers the literal plant_matched_nothing. I proved it from the failing side by aiming a plant at a pattern nothing matches, which dropped the control to 13 of 16 and named the file. That answers one of the 94 controls %519 counted, without touching the shape decision it left open.
obs The control also uses sed_inplace from tools/fixtures/s/shell_portable.sh rather than sed -i, since the two dialects disagree about what follows the flag. That raises the cure's adoption by one, against the 38 of 2,969 the card records.
obs Grading pushed one molt frame and I worked it down rather than filing it. My own edit took rota_declared_scan.sh from B/80 to C+/79 -- one negative sentence past the door, plus reach 70 on grade-12 prose. Rewriting it plainly, which is Gauge's first rule applied to my own comment, read B/83. Measured at molt time, closed at depth 2, stack unwound.
obs The card met its bound resolving all this: 40,906 with 54 bytes of headroom, and my entry wanted 995. Three rounds of trimming my own prose bought 10 bytes, which was the wrong instrument. Folding my two landed GRASS accounts to their own shelf -- the mechanism the card itself names -- brought it to 40,644, and left the card holding what is OPEN and what waits on Keaton's word.
obs A peer published %528 while I worked, so my rows renumbered before they were ever written; then the send's rebase brought five more peer rows, and the verify pass after it found two more still -- so my one surviving row moved 529, 530, 535, and finally %537. Four renumbers, each costing one command, because nothing outside the ledger had spelled a number. That is the derived spine's rule 4 working rather than being admired.

think THE LARGER HALF OF THIS LAP IS WITHDRAWN, and the rebase is what taught me. A peer booked the day-rollover red as %533 and closed it in the hours I was closing it here, with a repair that is better than mine: a day_shelf capability whose probe asks the guard's own question in BOTH halves -- the missing shelf and the shelf whose only log is untracked -- and skips by name. Mine taught the scan a third verdict, which is further from REDS %170 than refusing is. Sixteenth firing of two hands meeting on one line, and the second finder withdraws.
obs One half of mine survived because their repair could not reach it. Their witness reports ${ctl.err} on the control leg and ${scan.out} alone on the scan leg, so a scan refusal still wrote an empty reason into the roster's evidence file. One line, and the class stands: every witness in this tree that reports its scan's output alone throws the reason away.
obs And withdrawing cost almost nothing, which is the argument for measuring before defending. I had the peer's row, their probe, and my own scan side by side in one rebase, and reading their comment for two minutes settled it. What I kept is one line and one guard; what I dropped is a scan repair, five control cases and a set -eu near-miss that only existed because I wrote it.
obs And I wrote a stamp four minutes ahead of the clock. 20260907.005500 went into a fold, a shelf head, and two card lines before I read 20260907.005052 off the one clock. Swept all four sites to the stamp actually read. The earth row's own warning, in the lap that read it: a lap takes in a fabricated stamp the way a nose catches smoke.
think And the repair's own first run found the second face. The shelf existed by then -- I had just written this log into it -- yet the count reads git ls-files, so the day's FIRST log stays invisible until the lap commits it, and a lap runs its guards before its own commit by law. Same root, one step later, same discriminator, and the guard told me itself because the reason now reaches the reader.
obs The shared day_unopened helper returns 1 so the caller falls through to refuse, and a bare call under set -eu killed the script instead: the named-day case exited 1 with no message at all. Silence where a refusal belongs, in the lap whose whole subject is instruments that cannot say what they saw. Guarded with || : and planted both ways.
loom witness=rota_declared_control cases=16 failures=0 was=9
obs And the hot pass caught me under the very rule this lap was about. I cited two unshared numbers on the card while the anointed spine had bound only to 528, so unshared_citation read 2 in 1 file and standing_equipment reddened beneath it -- one root, two rows, the same shape as the red I opened the lap by closing. Rule 4 says cite by stamp until the spine binds the number; the card names the two stamps now. The gate fired before the push, which is what a gate is for, and it cost one line where the card records the unformed habit costing 28 errata.
obs CLAIMED, and named here because a claim is only reviewable if it is written down. The send's verify read four reds and two were peers': fold_shelf_link on construction/archive/20260907-013015_itinerary-landed-accounts.md, three links that kept the operator card's depth when they were folded one directory down, each resolving under exactly one correction; and reds_spine_derive on %530, double-booked on the anointed spine itself, which a peer has already booked as %536 and which I left standing since it is theirs to close. The three links are repaired here rather than left to block every ship's roster.
loom roster=hot_full_first guards_run=158 green=153 red=2 gated=3 seconds=1033 tree_moved=no
loom roster=hot_full_after_repair guards_run=158 green=155 red=0 gated=3 seconds=1066 tree_moved=no
loom roster=cold_scoped guards_run=128 green=124 red=2 gated=2 seconds=896 tree_moved=no
loom scan=rota_grid rows=5 cells=15 unresolved=0 sections_missing=0 seat_undeclared=1_before_0_after
loom witness=rota_grid_control cases=16 failures=0 disarmed_plant_proof=13_of_16
loom qa=earth_threshold before=A95 after=A94 setting=field
loom qa=rota_declared_scan elder=B80 mine=C79 repaired=B83
loom pin=ITINERARY before=40906 after=40644 bound=40960
loom pin=REDS peak=45915 after_fold=40334 bound=40960
rota_seats_agreeing 14 of 15
file tools/fixtures/r/rota_grid_scan.sh reads the grid's five rows and fifteen seats, gates unresolved, sections_missing and seat_undeclared at zero, refuses when the shape moves
file tools/fixtures/r/rota_grid_control.sh sixteen behaviors in a pen, every refusal planted then lifted, every plant proving it landed
file tools/r/rota_grid_witness.rish the five invariants, each saying why the reading it holds cannot be seen by a link wall
file tools/r/rota_declared_witness.rish the scan leg reports its reason too -- the half the peer's own repair left behind
file foundations/20260826-021735_earth-the-row-that-breathes-in.md the Cardinal section seats the living foundation and reaches the naming-law spec through it, keeping every true sentence and adding the mark half
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri rota_grid rostered tier lap, so the guard is heard
file construction/REDS.md one row booked at %537 after the spine moved four times under it, written straight to its shelf
file construction/archive/REDS-the-door-that-opened-on-the-seat-it-replaced-rows-537.md the shelf, carrying the finding and the withdrawal beside it
file construction/archive/20260907-005052_itinerary-landed-accounts.md my landed accounts folded off the card, so the live front holds what is open
file construction/ITINERARY.md the lap's line, two questions merged into one asked once, the prior-lap row moved
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- two findings are named and not taken, and both are about instruments that cannot see their own subject: a stderr-only refusal writes an empty reason into the roster's evidence, which is every witness rather than the one scan I fixed; and the report card cannot see staleness in three separate ways, which matters because this card tells every lap to let a grade decide whether a page needs work.
